  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/99d63eb31fba0757bde11dda3653e3791fcddb0b74b5325e20afcede4f3ea849.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/f7acd51dcca9ad48f4aa7ec026beaeb67ddc9b1e88ab9b5042016674ba6ea542.png)\n\nIn $\u25b3OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =a+4b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda a+\\mu b=1a+4b.$\n\nSince $a\u2226b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =4$",
